      "answer": "[1] The new framework introduces mandatory secure wrapping and labelling of sanitary waste. This is done to reduce vector attraction, pathogen transmission, and accidental exposure, particularly for informal waste handlers and sanitation workers. It also extends benefits to dignity, safety, and system professionalism, which are prerequisites for sustained compliance.\n\n[3] Health, Safety, and Occupational Risk Management SWM Rules, 2026 explicitly expand the compliance lens to include worker safety, a dimension historically under-reported in municipal systems. Recommended Indicators - Sanitary Waste Capture Rate (%): Percentage of sanitary waste safely segregated, collected, and disposed through authorised channels. - PPE Compliance Rate (%): Proportion of workers provided with and correctly using prescribed personal protective equipment at collection, transfer, processing, and disposal facilities. - Incident Frequency Rate: Recordable injuries or exposure incidents per 1,000 workers per annum. International alignment: These indicators mirror occupational safety benchmarks used in EU waste facilities and OECD reporting frameworks.\n\n[1] [3] Therefore, sanitary waste must be handled securely by being wrapped and clearly identified, reducing exposure risks and ensuring proper disposal. Proper handling of sanitary waste is crucial for infection control, worker safety, and system professionalism, which are essential for sustained compliance with the new framework.",
