      "question": "In the circular economy table, what does formally recognising MRFs achieve?",
      "answer": "[3]\nFormally recognizing Material Recovery Facilities (MRFs) in the circular economy table achieves several outcomes. Firstly, MRFs are formally recognised as sorting facilities and may also act as deposition points for multiple waste streams, including special care and sanitary waste, for further processing. Secondly, it strengthens the middle of the chain in the solid waste management system. Thirdly, it improves the post-collection sorting quality by controlling contamination, separating polymer and paper grades, removing fines and inserts, and managing rejection. Lastly, it ensures operational safeguards to keep MRFs credible, such as strict incoming segregation checks, recorded rejection ratios, fire prevention protocols, and worker safety measures. [3]",
